roposed scenarios). The planning documents show income statement first year combined regular income of £41,813 and combined expenditure of £24,001 in both scenarios (P12, P9).","discrepancies":["Report states total monthly net income £4,486.67; Fact Find shows total net monthly income £1,430.00 - a difference of £3,056.67 (214% variance).","Report breaks down monthly income as HGV £1,300 + Sea Cadets £86.67 + State Pension £1,000 + C2 Employment £1,100; Fact Find records only Employment £1,200 + State Pension £230 = £1,430, with no Sea Cadets or C2 Employment line items visible.","Report asserts monthly surplus of £2,786.67; Fact Find shows monthly disposable income of -£288.00 (a deficit of £3,074.67 difference).","Report states total monthly expenditure £1,700; Fact Find states 'In retirement, you expect your annual expenditure to be approximately £1,700' (P1) - this is annual, not monthly, creating a tenfold discrepancy in interpretation.","Report projects no shortfall in baseline scenario to age 100; intelliflo baseline scenario (P22, P5) shows declining assets after age 100 with deficits emerging in 2056-2059 (shortfalls of -£2,930, -£2,876, -£3,014, -£3,357) before exhaustion at age 100/96.","Report's cashflow assumptions include employment income (implicitly) as ongoing, but Fact Find 'Do you expect any changes in your income in the foreseeable future?' is marked 'No', and intelliflo timelines show 'Retire' events at 2026 (age 70/66) and 2027 (age 71/67) (P23), indicating employment income cessation.","Report states total assets £6,000; this matches Fact Find savings only, but excludes the £116,997 in accessible pensions that should be included in total liquid and accessible resources for affordability analysis."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The comparison reveals material inconsistencies between the report's financial position narrative and the underlying Fact Find and planning documents. The most critical discrepancy is the income figures. The report derives £4,486.67 monthly income from four sources, but the Fact Find evidences only £1,430 monthly, a shortfall of £3,056.67. The Sea Cadets income (£86.67) and C2 Employment (£1,100) are not visible in the Fact Find table (P2, P21), and the HGV employment is recorded at £1,200 (not £1,300). The State Pension figure of £1,000 monthly in the report does not align with the Fact Find's £230 monthly state pension entry. The report appears to have modelled £11,973 annual state pension (corroborated in P1, P12) which is approximately £998 monthly, closer to the reported £1,000 than the Fact Find's £230 entry. The expenditure error is equally grave: the report states £1,700 monthly expenditure, but the Fact Find and planning documents indicate this is annual retirement expenditure. The Fact Find shows monthly expenditure of £1,718 (P2), which produces a deficit not a surplus. The monthly surplus of £2,786.67 reported is arithmetically inconsistent with both sets of underlying figures. The Fact Find shows a monthly deficit of -£288; the planning documents' first-year combined income (£41,813) minus first-year combined expenditure (£24,001) yields approximately £1,501 monthly surplus, not £2,786.67. The cashflow scenarios in the report project no shortfall to age 100, but the intelliflo baseline scenario (P5, P22) explicitly shows deficits of -£2,930 to -£3,357 in years 2056-2059 (ages 100-103), directly contradicting the report's assertion of 'no shortfall that requires your pension or investment funds to be used' throughout retirement to age 100. The proposed scenario (P9, P12) does show stronger asset growth and remains positive to age 100, but this is for the proposed consolidated arrangement, not the current situation the baseline tests. The report's statement that 'all surplus income throughout your lifetime is spent, rather than saved' is supported by the 0% surplus savings assumption (P4, P17), and the planning documents confirm surplus accounts remain at £0 (P14, P23). The employment income modelled in the report appears to assume continuation at current levels (£300 per week for HGV plus £20 for Sea Cadets per P19), but the Fact Find does not list Sea Cadets employment, the planning timeline shows retirement events in 2026-2027 (P23), and the statement 'typically earning around £300 per week net' (P19) uses the qualifier 'typically', suggesting variability not reflected in deterministic inputs. The recommendation rests on the assertion that financial position supports contributions and that affordability is robust, but the foundational income and expenditure figures are demonstrably inconsistent with the Fact Find. The total assets figure of £6,000 cited excludes accessible pensions of £116,997, which materially understates the resources available. The consistency requirement demands that the report's figures be supported by the Fact Find; they are not.","citations":[{"passageId":"P2","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Total Net Monthly Income","£ 1,430.00"]},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Total Monthly Expenditure","£ 1,718.00"]},{"passageId":"P2","source":"Fact Find (Test 1).md","category":"B","quote":null,"cells":["Total Monthly Disposable Income","£ -288.00"]},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"In retirement, you expect your annual expenditure to be approximately £1,700","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"Kim receives £1,100 per month","cells":null},{"passageId":"P1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"You receive state pension income of £11,973 per annum","cells":null},{"passageId":"P19","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.md","category":"I","quote":"You receive £300 per week as an HGV driver","cells":null},{"passageId":"P5","source":"intelliflo planning Baseline scenari