ment and will not be available on the new plan","Report does not disclose the requirement for medical questionnaire to obtain replacement life cover and waiver benefits on a new plan","Report states all arrangements have no safeguarded benefits but does not address other material features being lost such as life cover and waiver protection on the Zurich plan","Report does not discuss potential loss or change of retirement access options (flexi access drawdown, UFPLS, annuity availability) between existing plans and new solution"],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The report asserts only that employer contributions will be lost. However, the evidence clearly shows that the Zurich arrangement includes life cover and waiver benefits that are explicitly noted in product literature [P3] as 'no longer available on new pension plans.' This is a material disadvantage that the report has failed to disclose. The Zurich policy information [P3] specifically warns: 'Both life cover and waiver benefits are no longer available on new pension plans. You would also need to complete a medical questionnaire to take out similar cover separate to your new pension plan. This will be particularly important if your health has deteriorated since taking out these benefits.' This is a clear, material benefit being given up. The report's statement that all arrangements 'do not contain any safeguarded benefits' is accurate but insufficient—safeguarded benefits are a narrow category; the report conflates absence of safeguarded benefits with absence of any material features. The obligation under COBS 9.4.7R and the regulatory framework requires fair, clear and accurate disclosure of 'all material disadvantages, risks, limitations...and consumer harms' including 'benefits, features or protections the client is giving up.' The loss of life cover and waiver protection, particularly where the client may have deteriorating health and replacement cover requires medical underwrit